1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
You want to put that up there towards the,

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:05,480
or you can just put it on the corner of yours right there.

3
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:07,040
All right, everyone, let's get started.

4
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,360
So everyone agrees for the PhD micro too?

5
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:10,600
I know, it's distributed.

6
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:13,080
You all agree, right?

7
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:14,040
98% says yes.

8
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:14,880
Okay, good.

9
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:15,700
All right, good.

10
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:20,700
All right, let's see.

11
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:49,640
Here, and you can go back to the corner.

12
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:53,220
So we've already violated one of Thomas Schelling's

13
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:54,660
fundamental laws of social science,

14
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:56,140
which is that in any room,

15
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:57,660
students will always sit as far away

16
00:00:57,660 --> 00:00:59,380
from the professor as possible.

17
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:01,540
For some reason, the front row is full,

18
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,300
and the rear rows are empty,

19
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:05,100
which I've never seen,

20
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:06,660
I don't think in my entire teaching career,

21
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:09,060
the only reason people sit in the front is normally

22
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:10,700
because there are no chairs left.

23
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:13,860
All right, so maybe this signals

24
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:15,700
that economics itself is gonna change

25
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:17,980
in the course of this course.

26
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:19,220
We shall see.

27
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:23,260
All right, so you remember all of the microeconomics

28
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:25,580
that Walter Williams taught you last semester?

29
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:27,620
This class, we do everything else.

30
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:29,180
Everything that he has not taught you,

31
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:30,620
we will now proceed to learn.

32
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:33,620
All right, so that means basic game theory,

33
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:35,220
information economics,

34
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:38,860
and then a whole lot of what have been called anomalies,

35
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:40,980
things that, at least on the surface,

36
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:44,260
do not fit with standard microeconomic theory.

37
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:46,140
All right, and then there's an underlying goal,

38
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:48,500
which educational psychology tells me

39
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:49,780
is highly unlikely to be achieved,

40
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:51,340
but I'm going to try anyway,

41
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:54,380
and that is to teach you how to think like economists.

42
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:57,420
There's a lot of research on teaching people how to think,

43
00:01:57,420 --> 00:01:59,020
which is very negative,

44
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:00,780
and I trust it because usually the people

45
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:02,060
who do the research begin saying,

46
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:03,260
I'm gonna show how it's possible

47
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:04,260
to teach people how to think,

48
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:06,780
and then they come away, I failed.

49
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:11,180
Anyway, we are going to pursue the quixotic quest

50
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:13,860
for economic understanding on a deep level.

51
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:15,500
I'm sorry, can you close the door?

52
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:18,100
Thank you.

53
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:19,300
All right, so prerequisites.

54
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:21,740
So I assume that you know multivariate calculus,

55
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:22,980
not the really hard stuff,

56
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,300
but how to differentiate an equation

57
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:26,340
with three variables in it.

58
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:28,420
So if you've forgotten that,

59
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:31,780
it's almost the same as doing one with two variables.

60
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,260
In fact, it's really similar.

61
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:37,380
Still don't know why the D is different

62
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:38,820
when you do it with multivariates.

63
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:40,420
I'm sure it's not one of the best, but.

64
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:41,500
All right, and then I also assume

65
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:43,420
you know intermediate micro.

66
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:46,300
So if you don't know intermediate micro currently,

67
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,340
this does not mean that you simply cannot take the course.

68
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:50,260
It does mean you should get an intermediate textbook

69
00:02:50,260 --> 00:02:53,740
and start reading it to get ahead of the game.

70
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:55,620
All right, then for texts.

71
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:59,300
We have a very old PhD micro text,

72
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:00,620
the very one that I used in grad school,

73
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:02,660
which so far I haven't seen one that's better.

74
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:04,020
I'm not saying it's great,

75
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:06,820
but I haven't seen one that is clearly better than this one,

76
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:08,380
so, and I've been using it for a while.

77
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:11,220
So David Krebs' Course in Microeconomic Theory.

78
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:15,020
I do think it is the least unreadable of all the main ones.

79
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:19,100
It's not really high praise, but there it is.

80
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,060
Then we've got two books by Steve Lansberg,

81
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:23,300
both of which are highly readable.

82
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:24,820
And one way you might think they're basic,

83
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:25,660
but on the other end,

84
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:27,620
I read these books in grad school and several times,

85
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:29,380
so now I understand what they were supposed

86
00:03:29,380 --> 00:03:30,220
to be teaching me.

87
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:33,220
So I think that they do actually do help you

88
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:34,940
think like economists.

89
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:36,820
Then we got Richard Thaler's book, Letters Curse,

90
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:40,500
and then Daniel Kahneman's Thinking, Vast and Slow.

91
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:42,620
And then there's also different readings

92
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:44,260
that are assigned throughout.

93
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:47,040
If there is a star, then they're available on JSTOR.

94
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:48,820
So you all know about JSTOR, right?

95
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:51,260
And you all know how to access journals

96
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:54,660
through library.gmu.edu.

97
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:58,940
So your lives are vastly better than mine was in your shoes.

98
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:02,620
We were supposed to go to the library's basement

99
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:07,460
and request a folder and then copy every page by hand,

100
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:11,460
just to avoid copyrights and to maximize transactions costs.

101
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,660
So anyway, you don't have to do that anymore.

102
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:16,900
Right, and then everything that is by me is on my webpage.

103
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:20,300
So you can go to econfaculty.gmu.edu

104
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:23,380
slash vcaplin slash cv.hnl.

105
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:25,080
Or if that's hard to remember,

106
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:28,620
you can also just go to www.vcaplin.com.

107
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:31,300
All right, so for this class.

108
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:34,520
So there's gonna be one midterm and one final exam.

109
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:40,180
And the midterm counts for 40% and the final counts for 45.

110
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:42,180
And the homework is the remaining 15.

111
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:43,660
Do not think this means that homework

112
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:45,460
is that important to your grade

113
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:50,460
because the variance in homework is not that large.

114
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:53,020
Right, so usually over half the students

115
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:54,260
can get full credit in the homework.

116
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:55,940
This is not true on the exams,

117
00:04:55,940 --> 00:04:57,900
which means the people who do not do the homework

118
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:01,900
will often lose a full grade or two grades in the class.

119
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:03,100
This is awkward sometimes.

120
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:04,140
Someone does great on the exams,

121
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:05,020
they don't do the homework,

122
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:08,100
and then they have to give you a C according to formula.

123
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:09,500
But anyway, so rather than,

124
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:12,340
I have already modified the formula a bit,

125
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:13,660
but this is the formula,

126
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:15,720
this is the way I will compute the grade.

127
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:18,500
And if it seems unjust, I'm gonna go with the formula.

128
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:19,660
So you have been warned.

129
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:22,860
So, and these weights are fixed.

130
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:24,580
So when someone does poorly on the midterm

131
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:27,140
and they ask me what can I do to improve my grade,

132
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:29,820
the formula implies the answer to the question.

133
00:05:29,820 --> 00:05:31,660
Do well on the homework and the final,

134
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:33,820
and then your grade will rise.

135
00:05:34,660 --> 00:05:37,400
Okay, then there's no formal grade for participation,

136
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:39,140
but at the end of the semester,

137
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:41,600
I go through everyone in the class

138
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:43,180
and make a subjective judgment

139
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:45,000
about whether you made this a better class

140
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:46,860
for the class overall.

141
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:47,820
And if the answer is yes,

142
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:49,720
then I bump you up one third of grade.

143
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:53,700
And then I also, since my memory

144
00:05:53,700 --> 00:05:55,260
may be getting worse my old age,

145
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:57,100
I think more likely I just accept

146
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,020
how my memory was always flawed.

147
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:01,940
I also tell, you don't need to remember this,

148
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:05,060
but the last class I will tell everyone,

149
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:07,460
if you believe you deserve credit for participation

150
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:09,780
and you fear I don't know your name,

151
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:12,060
email me a photograph of yourself.

152
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:15,740
And then this will prevent the injustice

153
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:19,060
that would otherwise occur if I can't link the two up.

154
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:22,700
Right, so anyway, I will tell you this again on the last day.

155
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:23,900
At least among undergraduates,

156
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:26,780
I'm shocked at how few you bother to take me up on this.

157
00:06:26,780 --> 00:06:28,300
So maybe they're just very humble,

158
00:06:28,300 --> 00:06:30,700
like, oh clearly I totally don't deserve anything.

159
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:34,740
Or maybe they just think I know everybody.

160
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:37,660
So, especially when we meet once a week,

161
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:38,800
that's actually not so likely,

162
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:41,900
although this class is unusually small this year.

163
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:45,420
So you know, we have like 23 students.

164
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:47,960
Earlier years we've been having like 35.

165
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:50,900
So this does improve your chances of being known,

166
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:53,900
but it does not raise them up to 100% or anything like that.

167
00:06:55,340 --> 00:06:58,060
Okay, then, so for homework,

168
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:00,820
there is a lot of homework for this class.

169
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:03,820
So I have a simple philosophy this class.

170
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:07,780
First of all, earning your PhD in microeconomics

171
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:10,140
should be the most important thing in your life.

172
00:07:10,140 --> 00:07:11,700
We all agree to that, right?

173
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:14,420
Secondly, microeconomics is the most important part

174
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:16,900
of economics, therefore this class

175
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:19,100
is now the most important thing in your life.

176
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:22,060
And as a result, we will be doing a lot of homework

177
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:24,780
in order to go and learn the enormous amount of material

178
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:26,420
that we still have to learn.

179
00:07:26,420 --> 00:07:29,900
There will be 10 homeworks, eight graded, two not graded.

180
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:32,620
There will be one that you will be given today

181
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:34,260
and will be due next class.

182
00:07:34,260 --> 00:07:36,780
In the syllabus it gives the deadlines

183
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:39,940
for all of the homeworks.

184
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:41,100
In the event of snow days,

185
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:42,980
which for about the past four years

186
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:44,980
there have been multiple snow days,

187
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:48,140
the week is determined by the number of weeks

188
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:49,180
that we have met.

189
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:51,300
So it's not based upon the actual calendar.

190
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:54,020
So if there is a blizzard next week,

191
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:57,820
then week two is two weeks from today.

192
00:07:57,820 --> 00:08:00,060
Right, so any official canceled snow day,

193
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:03,060
we just relabel what the week is.

194
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:07,220
So the syllabus is gold, but the week is malleable.

195
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:10,300
Does that mean we miss out on?

196
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:12,300
I mean, so if we miss days,

197
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:14,820
then normally what I will do is two things.

198
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:18,100
First, I will accelerate a small amount

199
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:20,660
and secondly, I will trim material

200
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:22,420
until we're back on track.

201
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:24,740
So I will not simply cut out the last week,

202
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:26,220
which I think is bad.

203
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:28,620
Was the last week so inferior to all the material

204
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:29,580
that we can just forget it?

205
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:32,420
No, instead I'll try to go and cut the marginal material

206
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:35,220
for each week until we are back on budget,

207
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:36,940
which that is the effort.

208
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:38,820
All right, and then on the homework,

209
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:40,740
unlike the exam, there are discrete grades.

210
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:41,980
So either get a check plus,

211
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:44,060
which is four points maximum credit,

212
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:45,580
check, which is three points,

213
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:47,380
check minus, which is two,

214
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:49,300
or if you don't turn it in, you get zero.

215
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:51,100
And then if the homework is late,

216
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:54,460
then you will lose one point.

217
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:55,780
If I've already given you the answer key,

218
00:08:55,780 --> 00:08:57,140
then you get zero.

219
00:08:57,140 --> 00:08:59,020
So that's, you're a good enough economist

220
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:01,020
to understand why after I hand out the answer key,

221
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:02,460
it is no longer, you can no longer get credit

222
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:04,060
for trying anything in.

223
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:06,580
All right, so let's see.

224
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:09,980
The best way to contact me is email,

225
00:09:09,980 --> 00:09:12,420
which is right here and should also, yes,

226
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:17,420
is up on the top left corner of the first page.

227
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:19,980
And then you can also satisfy a lot of requests

228
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:22,620
just by going to the class webpage.

229
00:09:22,620 --> 00:09:27,620
You get the easiest mnemonic is www.bcaplin.com.

